{"seq":10368,"at":"2026-10-23T01:03:26.752Z","actor":"agent","type":"node.proposed","payload":{"id":"werk-stack-ui","kind":"card","title":"The client builds its screens from one UI component system","why":"An agent writes most of the client. It writes faster and more consistently when it picks from a small set of known components than when it styles each screen by hand. The set must still let a screen change one component's look without a fork of the library.","body":"To keep screens consistent and cheap for an agent to write, the client uses one component system for buttons, inputs, dialogs, menus, tabs, tooltips and layout.

The system must meet four criteria. First, a small, fixed set of components, each with a documented contract, so an agent does not invent a new variant per screen. Second, a way to change one component's look or behaviour without a fork. Third, direct support for React, see #werk-stack-framework. Fourth, no style work at run time: styles come from static CSS, not from JavaScript that computes styles on each render.

Accessibility (keyboard focus, roles, screen reader labels) comes from the system, not from each screen.","status":"open","who":"you","parent":"werk-stack"}}
